<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kor River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kur River (Fars)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kor, or Kur, is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> Province in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, about 280 km long, which flows past the plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marvdasht">Marvdasht</a> to the salt lake of Bakhtegan. Its waters, joined by the Pulvar, irrigated the heartland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istakhr">Istakhr</a>. In the 10th century the Buyid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/band-e-amir-dam">Band-e Amir</a> dam on it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kor-river.jpg" alt="Kor River" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hadi Karimi · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paddies_around_Kor_River,_Kamfirouz_%D8%B4%D8%A7%D9%84%DB%8C%D8%B2%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%DB%8C_%D8%A7%D8%B7%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%81_%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%AF_%DA%A9%D8%B1%D8%8C_%DA%A9%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%81%DB%8C%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%B2_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
